{"name":"Little Ladybug","hex":"#ff1414","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"little","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Small, not large, limited, particularly:","Small, limited in size."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Not much.","Not at all."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/little"},{"term":"ladybug","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Synonym of ladybird."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ladybug"}],"entries":[{"title":"Coccinellidae","blocks":[{"text":"Coccinellidae is a widespread family of small beetles. They are commonly known as ladybugs in North America and ladybirds in the United Kingdom, Australia, New Zealand, Ireland, and other English speaking territories; \"lady\" refers to Mary, the mother of Jesus. Entomologists use the names ladybird beetles or lady beetles to avoid confusion with true bugs. The more than 6,000 described species have a global distribution and are found in a variety of habitats. They are oval beetles with a domed back and flat underside. Many of the species have conspicuous aposematic (warning) colours and patterns, such as red with black spots, that warn potential predators that they taste bad."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Coccinella-septempunctata-15-fws.jpg/330px-Coccinella-septempunctata-15-fws.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coccinellidae","wikidata":"Q25327"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/BIEDRONA_mirrored%2C_cropped.png/250px-BIEDRONA_mirrored%2C_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/BIEDRONA_mirrored%2C_cropped.png/500px-BIEDRONA_mirrored%2C_cropped.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/comm